data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider threw `InvalidOperationException` on synchronous LINQ by default but let you opt back in with sync-over-async. **In .NET 11, that opt-in is removed entirely.** All four of your sync calls will throw `InvalidOperationException` at runtime with no configuration workaround.

### Affected calls

| Sync (breaks) | Async replacement |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(...)` | `.FirstAsync(...)` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

### Fix — convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- `Add()` stays synchronous — it only tracks the entity in memory; no I/O happens until `SaveChangesAsync()`.
- Every caller up the chain must also become `async`/`await` (controllers, services, etc.).
- Remove any `.EnableSyncExecution()` or equivalent opt-in from your `DbContext` configuration — it will cause a build error since the API no longer exists.
